credentials.description:
Device: {device_id} at {host}

Enter the local key for this device. You can find it in the Tuya IoT developer portal, or with a tool such as tuya-cloudcutter.

Protocol version {version} is preselected, {version_source}. Correct it if you know better — the other versions are tried automatically if this one fails, and whichever works is the one that gets stored.

firmware_generation.description:
Different firmware generations of this doorbell use the same datapoint numbers for different things, so the names come from a table per generation. Without knowing which one applies, both tables are used at once and disagreements are resolved in favour of the newer — which is how a device can end up with two switches sharing a name.

Currently set to: {current}
Based on the datapoints this device reports: {suggested}

Only names that came from a table are changed. A name you set yourself is left alone.

progress.live_capture:
Capturing for {elapsed}s — {count} datapoint(s) seen.

{detail}

Event candidates so far: {events}

Press the doorbell, walk in front of it, and work through the Tuya app: toggle night vision, the chime, the indicator light and recording mode off and on again. A datapoint only appears once the device has reported it, and a setting nobody touches never does; switching each one twice puts it back where it was and reveals it at the same time. ⟳ marks a datapoint that has carried more than one value, which is what a button press and a motion sensor look like. ← marks whatever reported last.

**Close this dialog when you are done — that is how you stop, and nothing is lost.** The capture lives on the device, not in this window; reopen the options and choose 'Review capture results' to pick what to keep.

It also stops by itself after {idle}s without anything new, and after {limit} minutes regardless.
